window.FSINV_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"p": "index.html", "l": 2, "id": "features", "t": "Features", "s": "Overview", "x": "Parallel crawling: a work-stealing crawler with a configurable number of threads ( --threads ). Directories with millions of entries are stat'ed by several threads at once, and probe suggests a thread count for your storage. Compact Parquet output: about 25 bytes per entry with zstd (measured on 1.2 million real entries with 126-byte average paths), so a billion-file scan takes about 25 GB. Every part carries its scan ID and schema version. Lossless names: every name is kept byte for byte. Text columns are always valid UTF-8; when a name isn't valid UTF-8, or contains control characters, its exact bytes are kept as well. name_flags marks names that will cause trouble (legacy encodings, control characters, Windows-reserved names, and so on). Never stops on errors: an unreadable directory or vanished file is recorded in an errors table and the crawl continues. A thread stuck on a hung NFS ", "c": ["--max-memory", "--resolve-ids", "--stall-timeout", "--symlink-targets", "--threads", "fs-inventory resume"]}, {"p": "index.html", "l": 2, "id": "install", "t": "Install", "s": "Overview", "x": "On Linux or macOS: curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/robert-mcdermott/fs-inventory/main/install.sh | sh This installs the latest release as ~/.local/bin/fs-inventory . It checks the download's SHA-256 checksum and version, and replaces an existing copy only after both checks pass. It doesn't use sudo or change PATH . The Linux binaries are static, so they run on any distribution with kernel 3.2 or later, including RHEL 7. To build from source instead (Rust 1.88 or later): cargo build --release The user guide covers installing a pinned version, installing for every user, and building static Linux binaries.", "c": ["--proto", "--release", "--tlsv1"]}, {"p": "index.html", "l": 2, "id": "quick-start", "t": "Quick start", "s": "Overview", "x": "Scan a tree into a new output directory (Parquet with zstd by default): fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 Check that the scan is complete and consistent before using it: fs-inventory verify /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 Add subtree totals and file-age histograms: fs-inventory rollup /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 Query it in the DuckDB shell, using the macros in sql/fs_inventory.sql : .read sql/fs_inventory.sql SELECT kind, count(*) AS entries, sum(size) AS bytes FROM fs_entries('/scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25') GROUP BY kind; On a network file system, find a good thread count first (it writes nothing): fs-inventory probe /nfs/projects --threads 16,32,64,128,256 If a scan is interrupted or killed, continue it from its last checkpoint: fs-inventory resume /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 Only treat a scan as complete ", "c": ["--threads", "fs-inventory probe", "fs-inventory resume", "fs-inventory rollup", "fs-inventory scan", "fs-inventory verify", "fs_entries", "fs_inventory"]}, {"p": "index.html", "l": 2, "id": "documentation", "t": "Documentation", "s": "Overview", "x": "The documentation is also a website, with search: https://robert-mcdermott.github.io/fs-inventory/ . Document Contents User guide Installing; common scenarios (NFS, Lustre, scheduled scans, migrations); every command and option; the output formats, manifest, and exit codes; querying scans with DuckDB Schema Every column of every table (generated by fs-inventory schema --format markdown ) Architecture How fs-inventory works: the crawler, platform backends, output pipeline, and robustness sql/ DuckDB macros ( fs_inventory.sql ) and example queries ( examples.sql ) Changelog What each release contains PLAN.md Design history, decisions, and roadmap", "c": ["--format", "fs-inventory schema", "fs-inventory works", "fs_inventory"]}, {"p": "index.html", "l": 2, "id": "benchmarking", "t": "Benchmarking", "s": "Overview", "x": "bench/ has a turnkey benchmark for a server's storage that writes a report to send back, scale tests on generated trees of any size, a tree generator, and a runner that sweeps thread counts and compares against find .", "c": []}, {"p": "index.html", "l": 2, "id": "project-layout", "t": "Project layout", "s": "Overview", "x": "Path Contents crates/fs-inventory-core Library: walker, platform backends, name encoding, record model crates/fs-inventory Output writers and the fs-inventory command-line tool docs/ User guide, schema reference, and architecture sql/ DuckDB macros and example queries bench/ Benchmark kit", "c": ["fs-inventory command"]}, {"p": "index.html", "l": 2, "id": "license", "t": "License", "s": "Overview", "x": "Copyright 2026 Robert McDermott. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 . See NOTICE .", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "1-install", "t": "Install", "s": "User guide", "x": "fs-inventory is a single executable with no runtime dependencies. Each release has binaries for Linux (x86_64 and ARM) and macOS (Apple silicon and Intel). The Linux binaries are static: they run on any distribution with kernel 3.2 or later, including RHEL 7.", "c": ["fs-inventory is"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "install-a-release", "t": "Install a release", "s": "User guide · Install", "x": "curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/robert-mcdermott/fs-inventory/main/install.sh | sh The installer downloads the binary for your system and the release's SHA256SUMS file. It checks the binary's SHA-256 checksum, runs it to check its version, and only then installs it as ~/.local/bin/fs-inventory , replacing an existing copy in one step. It doesn't use sudo or change PATH . If ~/.local/bin isn't on your PATH , it says so and prints the full path to run. Pass options to the installer after sh -s -- : Option Environment variable Effect --version VERSION FS_INVENTORY_VERSION Install this release, such as v1.0.0 , instead of the latest --install-dir DIRECTORY FS_INVENTORY_INSTALL_DIR Install here instead of ~/.local/bin For example, to install a pinned version for every user of a server: curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.", "c": ["--check", "--install-dir", "--proto", "--tlsv1", "--version"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "build-from-source", "t": "Build from source", "s": "User guide · Install", "x": "Build with Rust 1.88 or later; the toolchain named in rust-toolchain.toml installs automatically through rustup. cargo build --release The binary is target/release/fs-inventory . Copy it anywhere on your PATH . A normal Linux build links against the system's glibc and runs only on systems with that glibc version or newer. To build a static binary like the released ones, build on a Linux machine (or in a Linux container) with the musl C tools installed ( musl-tools on Debian and Ubuntu): rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl The binary is target/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/release/fs-inventory . Use aarch64-unknown-linux-musl for ARM servers. The static build crawls as fast as the glibc build.", "c": ["--release", "--target"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "check-the-installation", "t": "Check the installation", "s": "User guide · Install", "x": "fs-inventory --version fs-inventory 1.0.0 On kernels older than 4.11 (such as RHEL 7), fs-inventory reads metadata with fstatat instead of statx , and btime (creation time) is always null.", "c": ["--version", "fs-inventory reads"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "2-quick-start", "t": "Quick start", "s": "User guide", "x": "Scan a directory tree into a new output directory: fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 fs-inventory: complete: 285 entries in 0:00:00 (33.3k/s), 35 dirs, 44.02 GiB, 1 error, 0 vanished; output: /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 Check that the scan is complete and consistent: fs-inventory verify /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 scan 01a1758c-51da-709d-bafb-c8feaa458bf3: complete, 285 entries in 1 part, 1 error in 1 part (parquet) ok Query it with DuckDB: duckdb -c \"SELECT kind, count(*) AS entries, sum(size) AS bytes FROM read_parquet('/scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25/entries/*.parquet') GROUP BY kind ORDER BY entries DESC\" ┌─────────┬─────────┬─────────────┐ │ kind │ entries │ bytes │ │ varchar │ int64 │ int128 │ ├─────────┼─────────┼─────────────┤ │ file │ 248 │ 47262948352 │ │ dir │ 36 │ 34749 │ │ symlink │ 1 │ 12 │ └─────────┴───", "c": ["fs-inventory scan", "fs-inventory verify"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "3-concepts", "t": "Concepts", "s": "User guide", "x": "A scan is one output directory. Each run of fs-inventory scan writes a new directory holding manifest.json and one subdirectory per table: /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25/ manifest.json scan ID, status, roots, options, totals, list of part files entries/part-w00-000000.parquet one row per file, directory, symlink, ... errors/part-w00-000000.parquet one row per failed operation (only if there were any) rollup/part-000000.parquet subtree totals per directory (after `fs-inventory rollup`) rollup_ages/part-000000.parquet file counts and bytes by age (after `fs-inventory rollup`) Tables are split into part files. A large scan writes many parts per table, so no single file grows without bound. DuckDB, Spark, and most Parquet readers read a glob such as entries/*.parquet as one table. Every entry gets a row. Files, directories, symlinks, devices, pipes, and sockets each get one row in en", "c": ["fs-inventory resume", "fs-inventory rollup", "fs-inventory scan", "fs-inventory verify"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "4-common-scenarios", "t": "Common scenarios", "s": "User guide", "x": "", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "41-inventory-a-directory-tree", "t": "Inventory a directory tree", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 fs-inventory verify /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 fs-inventory rollup /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 Use a new output directory for each scan. scan refuses to write into a directory that already contains files: fs-inventory: output directory /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 is not empty; choose a new directory for each scan Name scan directories by tree and date, such as projects-2026-10-25 . Scans then sort by date, and several can be queried together ( 7.5 ). The output directory can sit inside the scanned tree. fs-inventory skips its own output, so it never inventories itself. rollup is optional. It adds subtree totals and age histograms ( 4.9 ). It reads the finished scan, so it can run at any time later.", "c": ["fs-inventory rollup", "fs-inventory scan", "fs-inventory skips", "fs-inventory verify"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "42-scan-a-large-network-file-system", "t": "Scan a large network file system", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "NFS and other network file systems answer each metadata request over the network, so a single thread spends most of its time waiting. More threads keep more requests in flight. Find a good thread count with probe before the first big scan: fs-inventory probe /nfs/projects --threads 16,32,64,128,256 --duration 30s It crawls the tree for up to 30 seconds at each thread count, writes nothing, and suggests the smallest count that reaches 90% of the best rate ( 5.5 ). Then scan with that count, and with the options a long unattended scan needs: fs-inventory scan /nfs/projects \\ -o /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 \\ --threads 128 \\ --stall-timeout 10m \\ --progress json --progress-interval 60s \\ 2>> /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25.log --threads 128 : network file systems usually do best between 64 and 256. The default is 32. --stall-timeout 10m : if a crawl thread is stuck for 10 m", "c": ["--duration", "--max-memory", "--progress", "--progress-interval", "--stall-timeout", "--statx-dont-sync", "--threads", "fs-inventory probe", "fs-inventory resume", "fs-inventory scan"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "43-fast-namespace-only-scans-on-lustre", "t": "Fast namespace-only scans on Lustre", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "On Lustre, a file's size and allocated blocks live on the object storage servers, so reading them costs an extra round trip per file. When you only need names, owners, and times, skip them: fs-inventory scan /lustre/projects -o /scratch/inventory/lustre-names-2026-10-25 --fields -size,-blocks Skipped fields are written as null ( size and alloc_bytes here), and on Linux they aren't requested from the kernel at all. --fields accepts: Value Reads (not given) Everything: size,blocks,atime,mtime,ctime,btime -size,-blocks Everything except size and blocks mtime,ctime Only these optional fields times atime,mtime,ctime,btime Names, types, owners, permissions, inode numbers, and link counts are always read.", "c": ["--fields", "fs-inventory scan"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "44-several-roots-bind-mounts-and-loops", "t": "Several roots, bind mounts, and loops", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "Scan several trees into one inventory by listing them all: fs-inventory scan /data/projects /data/archive /home -o /scratch/inventory/all-2026-10-25 Each root gets depth 0 and parent_id 0. Without symlink following (fs-inventory never follows symlinks), a directory can be reached twice only through a bind mount, a hard-linked directory (some old file systems allow them), or roots that overlap ( /data and /data/projects ). If any of those are possible, add --detect-cycles : fs-inventory scan /data /data/projects -o /scratch/inventory/data-2026-10-25 --detect-cycles It remembers every directory entered, by device and inode, and never enters one twice. A directory reached again gets a row with error set to revisit: already visited through another path , and the manifest counts revisits. It costs about 50 bytes of memory per directory.", "c": ["--detect-cycles", "fs-inventory never", "fs-inventory scan"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "45-excluding-paths", "t": "Excluding paths", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 \\ --exclude node_modules --exclude '*.tmp' --exclude '/data/projects/*/scratch' A glob without / matches a name at any depth: node_modules , *.tmp , .git . A glob with / matches whole paths: /data/projects/*/scratch . --exclude-from FILE reads globs from a file, one per line. Both options can be repeated. Quote globs so the shell doesn't expand them. Excluded entries get no row, aren't counted in their parent directory's totals, and excluded directories aren't entered. In the demo tree, --exclude node_modules dropped the scan from 285 entries to 196. Two kinds of directory are skipped without any option: Snapshot directories ( .snapshot , ~snapshot , .snapshots , .zfs ), which would otherwise multiply the inventory by the number of snapshots. Add --include-snapshots to scan them. The scan's own output directory.", "c": ["--exclude", "--exclude-from", "--include-snapshots", "fs-inventory scan"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "46-depth-limits-and-mount-points", "t": "Depth limits and mount points", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "--max-depth N lists entries down to N levels below each root. Directories at depth N get a row but aren't read, so their dir_* totals are null: fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o - -f csv --columns path,kind,depth,dir_entries --max-depth 1 path,kind,depth,dir_entries /data/projects/genomics,dir,1, /data/projects/imaging,dir,1, /data/projects/webapp,dir,1, /data/projects/shared,dir,1, /data/projects/restricted,dir,1, /data/projects,dir,0,5 Mount points are directories on a different file system from their parent. They're always recorded, with is_mount_point true, but not entered unless you add --cross-mounts . fs-inventory never triggers automounts.", "c": ["--columns", "--cross-mounts", "--max-depth", "fs-inventory never", "fs-inventory scan"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "47-scheduled-scans-for-a-database-or-web-application", "t": "Scheduled scans for a database or web application", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "For regular scans that feed a database or an application, run scan, verify, and rollup from a script, and load only scans that verify. This one runs nightly from cron: #!/bin/bash # /usr/local/bin/inventory-projects: nightly inventory of /data/projects. set -u root=/data/projects dir=/scratch/inventory/projects-$(date +%F) fs-inventory scan \"$root\" -o \"$dir\" --threads 64 --stall-timeout 30m \\ --progress json --progress-interval 5m 2>> \"$dir.log\" status=$? # 0: complete; 1: complete, but some entries couldn't be read (see errors/). if [ \"$status\" -gt 1 ]; then echo \"scan of $root failed with exit code $status; see $dir.log\" >&2 exit \"$status\" fi fs-inventory verify \"$dir\" || exit 1 fs-inventory rollup \"$dir\" || exit 1 echo \"$dir is ready to load\" If a scan is interrupted, such as by a reboot (exit code 3, or a manifest left at status running ), fs-inventory resume \"$dir\" finishes it. # m ", "c": ["--fields", "--progress", "--progress-interval", "--stall-timeout", "--threads", "fs-inventory resume", "fs-inventory rollup", "fs-inventory scan", "fs-inventory verify", "fs-inventory version", "fs_diff", "fs_inventory"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "48-streaming-to-another-program", "t": "Streaming to another program", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "With -o - , CSV or JSON Lines rows go to stdout, and error rows go to stderr as text lines: fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o - -f jsonl | gzip > projects.jsonl.gz fs-inventory scan /data/projects/shared -o - -f csv --columns path,kind,size,mtime,uid,name_flags,path_b64 | head -5 path,path_b64,name_flags,kind,uid,size,mtime /data/projects/shared/Résumé.pdf,,0,file,1004,1024,2023-06-01T12:00:00.000000Z /data/projects/shared/数据.csv,,0,file,1004,1024,2023-06-01T12:00:00.000000Z /data/projects/shared/naïve notes.txt,,0,file,1004,1024,2023-06-01T12:00:00.000000Z /data/projects/shared/CON,,512,file,1004,1024,2023-06-01T12:00:00.000000Z Errors on stderr look like this: fs-inventory: open /data/projects/restricted: Permission denied (os error 13) Columns come out in schema order, whatever order --columns lists them in. Parquet can't be streamed to stdout. Use an output directory for Parquet. I", "c": ["--columns", "fs-inventory scan"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "49-subtree-totals-and-file-age-heat-maps", "t": "Subtree totals and file-age heat maps", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "Directory rows count only their immediate children. To answer \"how big is this project, including everything below it?\", run rollup on the finished scan: fs-inventory rollup /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 rollup: 36 directories, 149 age rows (depth <= 4); wrote rollup/ and rollup_ages/ in /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 note: 1 directory wasn't read completely, so some totals are lower bounds (see incomplete_dirs) It writes two tables into the scan: rollup : one row per directory with totals over its whole subtree: entries, files, directories, bytes, allocated bytes, the oldest and newest modification times, and incomplete_dirs . That last one counts directories in the subtree that weren't fully read (unreadable, not entered, or past --max-depth ); when it isn't 0, the totals are lower bounds. rollup_ages : for each directory down to --age-depth levels (default 4), file co", "c": ["--age-buckets", "--age-depth", "--max-depth", "fs-inventory rollup"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "410-finding-names-that-will-break-a-migration", "t": "Finding names that will break a migration", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "Before moving data to Windows, SMB shares, object storage, or a new file system, find names that won't survive the move: SELECT path, fs_name_flags(name_flags) AS problems, coalesce(path_raw, encode(path)) AS exact_bytes FROM fs_entries('/scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25') WHERE name_flags <> 0 ORDER BY path; ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ path │ problems │ exact_bytes │ │ varchar │ varchar[] │ blob │ ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ /data/projects/shared/ leading space.txt │ [leading_trailing_space] │ /data/projects/shared/ leading space.txt │ │ /data/projects/shared/-rf │ [leading_dash] │ /data/projects/shared/-rf │ │ /data/projects/shared/CON │ [windows_reserved] │ /data/projects/shared/CON │ │ /data/projects/sha", "c": ["fs_entries", "fs_name_flags"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "411-text-output-for-spreadsheets-and-other-tools", "t": "Text output for spreadsheets and other tools", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "Parquet is the default and the best choice for DuckDB and data tools. For tools that only read text, write CSV or JSON Lines instead: fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o /scratch/inventory/projects-csv -f csv --compression zstd /scratch/inventory/projects-csv/ manifest.json entries/part-w00-000000.csv.zst errors/part-w00-000000.csv.zst Text compression: CSV and JSON Lines can be compressed with zstd or gzip , or left uncompressed (the default). Timestamps: written as ISO-8601 UTC text by default; --time-format epoch writes seconds since 1970 instead, for tools that want numbers. The DuckDB macros read ISO times. In JSON Lines, epoch times are JSON numbers, which most readers parse as doubles, keeping about a quarter of a microsecond: use ISO times or Parquet when nanoseconds matter. Fewer columns: --columns keeps only the columns you list, in any format. Loading into DuckDB: the fs_entri", "c": ["--columns", "--compression", "--time-format", "fs-inventory convert", "fs-inventory scan", "fs_entries_csv", "fs_entries_jsonl"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "412-interrupted-crashed-and-stalled-scans", "t": "Interrupted, crashed, and stalled scans", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "Every scan into a directory takes checkpoints, every 10 minutes by default ( --checkpoint-interval ). A checkpoint pauses the crawl for a moment, makes every row written so far durable, and records the directories still to read, in checkpoint/ inside the scan directory. That directory is removed when the scan finishes. Ctrl-C (SIGINT) or SIGTERM stops a scan gracefully. The threads finish the directories they're reading, the part files are completed, a final checkpoint is written, and the manifest gets status interrupted . The exit code is 3. A second signal exits at once, with code 130, leaving the manifest at status running and the last periodic checkpoint in place. To continue a scan that was interrupted or killed, ran out of memory, or was on a machine that went down, run resume on its directory: fs-inventory resume /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 For the demo scan, stopped pa", "c": ["--checkpoint-interval", "--detect-cycles", "--max-memory", "--stall-timeout", "--threads", "--writers", "fs-inventory resume", "fs-inventory watches"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "413-permissions-and-side-effects", "t": "Permissions and side effects", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "Run as a user who can read the whole tree (often root) for a complete inventory. Directories the user can't read are recorded with error set to open: EACCES and an errors row, and everything else is still scanned. Read-only: fs-inventory never opens files, only directories, so file access times don't change. On Linux it opens directories with O_NOATIME when allowed (the owner, or root), so directory access times don't change either. No symlinks are followed. A symlink gets its own row; its size is the length of the target path. With --symlink-targets , symlink_target records where it points, at the cost of one more call per symlink. No automounts are triggered, and other file systems aren't entered without --cross-mounts .", "c": ["--cross-mounts", "--symlink-targets", "fs-inventory never"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "414-user-and-group-names", "t": "User and group names", "s": "User guide · Common scenarios", "x": "Entries record numeric user and group IDs. To have names too, add --resolve-ids : fs-inventory scan /data/projects -o /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 --resolve-ids The scan directory then has two more tables, in the scan's format: users , with a uid and name for every user ID in entries , and groups , with a gid and name for every group ID. An ID with no account on the scanning host, such as a deleted user's, gets a null name. Looked up once per ID. Each crawl thread notes the IDs it sees, at almost no cost, and the names are looked up once the scan finishes: thousands of IDs take seconds, however many files they own. On Linux this uses getent , which asks every source the host is configured for (local files, LDAP, sssd, NIS), so it works from the static binary. On macOS it uses dscacheutil . Names as the scanning host sees them, when the scan finishes. Run the scan on a host that", "c": ["--resolve-ids", "fs-inventory scan", "fs_entries_named"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "5-command-reference", "t": "Command reference", "s": "User guide", "x": "fs-inventory scan ROOT... -o DIR|- Crawl directory trees and write an inventory fs-inventory verify SCAN_DIR Check that a scan is complete and consistent fs-inventory rollup SCAN_DIR Add subtree totals and age histograms to a scan fs-inventory schema Print the inventory schema fs-inventory probe PATH Time crawls at several thread counts fs-inventory resume SCAN_DIR Continue an interrupted scan from its checkpoint fs-inventory convert SCAN_DIR -f F Re-export a Parquet scan as CSV or JSON Lines fs-inventory COMMAND --help lists every option. Sizes accept K , M , G , T (or KiB , MiB , GiB , TiB ) for powers of 1024 and KB , MB , GB , TB for powers of 1000, such as 512MiB . Durations accept forms such as 30s , 10m , 1h 30m , 7d , and 1y .", "c": ["--help", "fs-inventory convert", "fs-inventory probe", "fs-inventory resume", "fs-inventory rollup", "fs-inventory scan", "fs-inventory schema", "fs-inventory verify"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "51-scan", "t": "scan", "s": "User guide · Command reference", "x": "fs-inventory scan [OPTIONS] --output <DIR|-> <ROOT>... Roots are made absolute (relative paths are taken from the current directory) and otherwise kept as given. Each root that's a directory is crawled, including a root that's a symlink to a directory; a root that's a file gets one row. Below the roots, symlinks are never followed. Output Option Default Effect -o, --output DIR|- (required) New output directory, or - to stream CSV or JSON Lines to stdout -f, --format parquet parquet , csv , or jsonl --compression CODEC zstd:3 for Parquet, none for text zstd , zstd:LEVEL (1–22), gzip , gzip:LEVEL (1–9), snappy , lz4 , or none . Text formats take only zstd , gzip , or none . --columns LIST all Write only these entry columns, such as path,size,mtime,uid --part-max-bytes SIZE 1GiB Start a new part file after this many bytes --part-max-rows N none Start a new part file after this many rows --r", "c": ["--checkpoint-interval", "--columns", "--compression", "--cross-mounts", "--csv-raw-controls", "--detect-cycles", "--exclude", "--exclude-from", "--fields", "--format", "--include-snapshots", "--max-depth", "--max-memory", "--output", "--part-max-bytes", "--part-max-rows"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "52-verify", "t": "verify", "s": "User guide · Command reference", "x": "fs-inventory verify /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 Checks a scan before it's used or loaded anywhere: Manifest: it can be read; its schema version is the one this fs-inventory writes; its status is complete . Part files: every part the manifest lists exists with the recorded size and row count, and each Parquet part carries the scan's ID. Totals: row counts match the manifest's totals. Stray files: none are left over, such as .tmp files from a crash. Rollup: its tables are checked the same way, if present. It prints a summary and ok , and exits with code 0. Otherwise it prints each problem to stderr and exits with code 1: fs-inventory: problem: scan status is `interrupted`, not `complete`", "c": ["fs-inventory verify", "fs-inventory writes"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "53-rollup", "t": "rollup", "s": "User guide · Command reference", "x": "fs-inventory rollup [--age-buckets LIST] [--age-depth N] SCAN_DIR Option Default Effect --age-buckets LIST 30d,90d,1y,2y,3y,5y,10y Age bucket edges, ascending, measured back from the scan's start time --age-depth N 4 Compute age histograms for directories down to this depth (0 is the root). Each costs about 0.5 KB of memory. With the default buckets, the labels are future (newer than the scan's start), <30d , 30d-90d , 90d-1y , 1y-2y , 2y-3y , 3y-5y , 5y-10y , and >=10y . A year is 365.25 days. The manifest gains a rollup section recording the buckets, depth, and part files. See 4.9 .", "c": ["--age-buckets", "--age-depth", "fs-inventory rollup"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "54-schema", "t": "schema", "s": "User guide · Command reference", "x": "fs-inventory schema # DuckDB CREATE TABLE statements fs-inventory schema --format json # a JSON description of every column fs-inventory schema --format markdown # the reference in docs/schema.md", "c": ["--format", "fs-inventory schema"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "55-probe", "t": "probe", "s": "User guide · Command reference", "x": "fs-inventory probe [--threads LIST] [--duration DURATION] [--drop-caches] PATH Crawls PATH at each thread count for up to --duration (default 30 s each), writes nothing, and suggests the smallest thread count within 90% of the best rate. More threads past that point mostly add load on the file server. fs-inventory probe /data/projects --threads 1,2,4 --duration 2s Probing /data/projects for up to 0:00:02 per thread count. Without --drop-caches (Linux, root), later runs read metadata cached by earlier ones. threads entries time entries/s 1 285 0:00:00 211.2k (read the whole tree) 2 285 0:00:00 332.7k (read the whole tree) 4 285 0:00:00 183.4k (read the whole tree) Suggested: --threads 2 Caching skews later runs. Each run after the first finds metadata the earlier runs left in the kernel's cache, so it looks faster than a real scan would be. On Linux, run as root with --drop-caches to clea", "c": ["--drop-caches", "--duration", "--threads", "fs-inventory probe"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "56-resume", "t": "resume", "s": "User guide · Command reference", "x": "fs-inventory resume [OPTIONS] SCAN_DIR Continues a scan from its last checkpoint; see 4.12 . Everything that shapes the output comes from the original scan. These options change how it runs; each defaults to how the scan ran: Option Effect -t, --threads N Crawl threads --writers N Most encoder and writer threads --checkpoint-interval DURATION Interval between checkpoints ( 0 disables them) --stall-timeout DURATION Give up on stalled directories after this long ( 0 waits forever) --max-memory SIZE Memory budget for the scan --progress , --progress-interval As for scan (these don't carry over) resume exits like scan ( exit codes ). A scan that's already complete, or finished except for stalled directories, has nothing to resume: resume says so and exits with code 0. It exits with code 2 if the scan has no checkpoint (it was run with --checkpoint-interval 0 ), or if another fs-inventory pro", "c": ["--checkpoint-interval", "--max-memory", "--progress", "--progress-interval", "--stall-timeout", "--threads", "--writers", "fs-inventory process", "fs-inventory resume"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "57-convert", "t": "convert", "s": "User guide · Command reference", "x": "fs-inventory convert [OPTIONS] --format <csv|jsonl> --output <DIR|-> <SCAN_DIR> Re-exports a Parquet scan as CSV or JSON Lines, for tools that only read text. The result is what the same scan would have written in that format: the same columns, escaping, path_b64 exact bytes, and time formatting, taken from the scan's own settings ( --columns , --fields , --raw-paths , --csv-raw-controls , --time-unit , and --time-format ). Option Effect -f, --format csv|jsonl Output format, as for scan . ( --to , its name in 0.6.0, still works.) -o, --output DIR|- A new or empty directory, laid out like a scan with its own manifest, or - to stream entries to stdout (errors go to stderr, as for scan -o - ) --compression CODEC zstd , zstd:LEVEL , gzip , gzip:LEVEL , or none (the default) Both tables: entries and errors are converted; rollup tables aren't. The manifest is the scan's own, with the new forma", "c": ["--columns", "--compression", "--csv-raw-controls", "--fields", "--format", "--output", "--raw-paths", "--time-format", "--time-unit", "--to", "fs-inventory convert"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "6-output-reference", "t": "Output reference", "s": "User guide", "x": "", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "part-files", "t": "Part files", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "Naming: each table directory holds parts named part-wNN-NNNNNN.EXT , where wNN is the writer thread and the second number counts that writer's parts. Parts written after a resume are part-rN-wNN-NNNNNN.EXT , where rN counts the resumes. Rollup parts are part-NNNNNN.parquet . Extensions: .parquet ; .csv or .jsonl , plus .zst or .gz when compressed. Rolling: a part is closed and a new one started at --part-max-bytes (1 GiB by default) or --part-max-rows . Atomic commits: a part is written as NAME.tmp , flushed to disk, and only then renamed. A part without .tmp is always complete, and after a crash only .tmp files are left to clean up. One file per table for small scans: writer threads start only as output backs up. Each checkpoint also commits the open parts, so a scan running for hours has more, smaller parts.", "c": ["--part-max-bytes", "--part-max-rows"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "the-checkpoint-directory", "t": "The checkpoint directory", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "While a scan runs, and after it's interrupted, its directory also holds checkpoint/ : File Contents state The latest checkpoint: the scan's settings, every committed part, and the directories still to read spill Directories waiting to be read that didn't fit in memory ( 8.3 ) visited With --detect-cycles , the directories entered so far lock Locked by the process writing the scan, so two can't write one scan at once It's removed when the scan finishes. A scan that still has it isn't finished; check the manifest's status .", "c": ["--detect-cycles"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "formats", "t": "Formats", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "Parquet (default) CSV JSON Lines Best for DuckDB, Spark, pandas, and other data tools Spreadsheets and tools that read only CSV Log pipelines and document stores Size About 25 bytes per entry Several times larger Larger still Types Stored in the file Text; use the _csv macros Text; use the _jsonl macros Exact name bytes path_raw (binary) path_b64 (base64 text) path_b64 (base64 text) Timestamps TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE , UTC ISO-8601 UTC text or epoch seconds ISO-8601 UTC text or epoch seconds Compression zstd level 3; any codec none, zstd, or gzip none, zstd, or gzip Stream to stdout No Yes Yes Parquet files use zstd compression, row groups of up to 1,048,576 rows or 128 MiB, and page statistics, so DuckDB can skip row groups when filtering on mtime , size , or uid . Each file's metadata records the scan ID, schema version, tool version, host, and roots.", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "names-and-exact-bytes", "t": "Names and exact bytes", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "path , parent_path , and name are always valid UTF-8. How a name is written depends on the format: Name bytes Parquet JSON Lines CSV Valid UTF-8 As is As is As is Control characters (newline, tab, ...) As is JSON escapes ( \\n ) \\n , \\t , \\r , or \\xNN ; as is with --csv-raw-controls Not valid UTF-8 Each bad byte as \\xNN Each bad byte as \\xNN Each bad byte as \\xNN Whenever the text differs from the exact bytes, those bytes are also stored: in path_raw in Parquet, and base64-encoded in path_b64 in CSV and JSON Lines. Symlink targets ( --symlink-targets ) follow the same rules, in symlink_target with symlink_target_raw or symlink_target_b64 . In every format, coalesce(path_raw, encode(path)) is the exact path (the text macros decode path_b64 into path_raw for you). With --raw-paths always , every row gets the exact bytes. For example, a name with a newline in CSV: path,path_b64 /data/project", "c": ["--csv-raw-controls", "--raw-paths", "--symlink-targets", "fs_name_flags"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "timestamps", "t": "Timestamps", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "Stored in UTC, with microsecond precision by default ( --time-unit ns for nanoseconds). ctime is not creation time. It's the last change to the contents or metadata, and users can't set it, so it's the reliable signal of recent activity. mtime can mislead. Copies, restores, tar , and rsync -t preserve it, so restored data can look years old. btime is creation time, when the platform and file system record it: on Linux with kernel 4.11 or later and a file system that stores it, and on macOS. time_flags marks timestamps that couldn't be represented and were clamped.", "c": ["--time-unit"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "nulls", "t": "Nulls", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "Column Null when size , alloc_bytes , atime , mtime , ctime , btime Skipped with --fields btime Not available (see above) dir_id The entry isn't a directory dir_entries and the other dir_* totals The entry isn't a directory, or the directory wasn't read (unreadable, past --max-depth , an unentered mount point) extension No extension, or a directory path_raw / path_b64 path is already exact rdev Not a device error Nothing went wrong", "c": ["--fields", "--max-depth"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "errors-and-vanished-entries", "t": "Errors and vanished entries", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "The errors table has one row per failed operation: open , readdir , stat , verify (the directory was replaced during the scan), stall (given up on after --stall-timeout ), or internal (a bug in fs-inventory, contained to one entry). Each row has the path, the OS error number and name (such as EACCES ), a message, and the time. The table exists only if there were errors. Entries deleted between being listed and being read, including directories deleted while they're being read, are counted as vanished in the manifest and the progress output. They aren't errors: live file systems change during a scan.", "c": ["--stall-timeout"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "the-manifest", "t": "The manifest", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "manifest.json describes the scan. From the demo scan, with lists shortened: { \"columns\": [\"path\", \"parent_path\", \"name\", \"...\", \"error\"], \"compression\": \"zstd:3\", \"duration_seconds\": 0.008556169, \"error_columns\": [\"path\", \"path_raw\", \"op\", \"errno\", \"errno_name\", \"message\", \"time\"], \"errors_by_kind\": [ { \"count\": 1, \"errno\": 13, \"errno_name\": \"EACCES\", \"op\": \"open\" } ], \"finished_at\": \"2026-10-26T00:11:32.707136Z\", \"format\": \"parquet\", \"host\": \"bd65838ce5c7\", \"options\": { \"threads\": 32, \"writers\": 5, \"exclude\": [], \"max_depth\": null, \"...\": \"...\" }, \"parts\": { \"entries\": [ { \"bytes\": 16233, \"file\": \"entries/part-w00-000000.parquet\", \"rows\": 285 } ], \"errors\": [ { \"bytes\": 2553, \"file\": \"errors/part-w00-000000.parquet\", \"rows\": 1 } ] }, \"rollup\": { \"age_buckets\": [\"future\", \"<30d\", \"...\", \">=10y\"], \"age_depth\": 4, \"...\": \"...\" }, \"roots\": [ { \"path\": \"/data/projects\", \"path_b64\": null } ],", "c": ["--detect-cycles", "--resolve-ids", "--stall-timeout", "fs-inventory version"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "progress-output", "t": "Progress output", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "With --progress plain , a line every --progress-interval : fs-inventory: 0:00:02 284.2k entries (140.0k/s) 151 dirs 0 B 0 errors 40 queued That is: elapsed time, entries so far (and the rate since the last line), directories read, bytes seen (the sum of size over everything but directories), errors, and directories waiting to be read. When threads are stalled, the line ends with how many and the longest-stalled directory. With --progress json , one object per line, with the same values: {\"bytes\":0,\"dirs\":202,\"elapsed_seconds\":2.001,\"entries\":382202,\"entries_per_second\":188013.0,\"errors\":0,\"event\":\"progress\",\"queued\":29,\"stalled\":[],\"vanished\":0} Every scan ends with a summary, unless --progress none : fs-inventory: complete: 285 entries in 0:00:00 (33.3k/s), 35 dirs, 44.02 GiB, 1 error, 0 vanished; output: /scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25 With --progress json , the summary is an ob", "c": ["--progress", "--progress-interval"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "exit-codes", "t": "Exit codes", "s": "User guide · Output reference", "x": "Code scan and resume verify Other commands 0 Complete with no errors (or, for resume , nothing to resume) The scan is good Success 1 Complete, but some entries couldn't be read (see errors ) Problems found — 2 Invalid arguments or configuration Invalid arguments Invalid arguments 3 Interrupted, or gave up on stalled directories — — 4 Fatal: output couldn't be written — Failed (such as rollup on an unreadable scan) 130 A second Ctrl-C or SIGTERM — —", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "7-querying-scans-with-duckdb", "t": "Querying scans with DuckDB", "s": "User guide", "x": "DuckDB is an analytical database that runs as a single program and queries Parquet, CSV, and JSON files in place. It's the quickest way to answer questions about a scan, and it handles billions of rows on a laptop. The examples use DuckDB 2.0.", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "71-reading-a-scan-directly", "t": "Reading a scan directly", "s": "User guide · Querying scans with DuckDB", "x": "No setup is needed for Parquet scans: SELECT count(*) FROM read_parquet('/scratch/inventory/projects-2026-10-25/entries/*.parquet');", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "72-the-macros", "t": "The macros", "s": "User guide · Querying scans with DuckDB", "x": "sql/fs_inventory.sql defines table macros, which are named queries that take the scan directory as an argument. They save typing the glob, and for text scans they apply the right column types. Macro Reads fs_entries(scan_dir) The entries table of a Parquet scan fs_errors(scan_dir) The errors table of a Parquet scan. It fails with \"No files found\" if the scan had no errors. fs_users(scan_dir) , fs_groups(scan_dir) The users and groups tables of a Parquet scan made with --resolve-ids fs_entries_named(scan_dir) fs_entries plus user_name and group_name , for a Parquet scan made with --resolve-ids fs_diff(old_dir, new_dir) What changed between two Parquet scans: one row per added, removed, or changed entry, with changes listing the columns that differ and the rows before and after as old and new structs ( 7.5 ) fs_diff_summary(old_dir, new_dir) fs_diff counted by change and kind, with the net", "c": ["--columns", "--resolve-ids", "fs-inventory rollup", "fs_diff", "fs_diff_summary", "fs_entries", "fs_entries_csv", "fs_entries_jsonl", "fs_entries_named", "fs_errors", "fs_errors_csv", "fs_errors_jsonl", "fs_groups", "fs_groups_csv", "fs_groups_jsonl", "fs_inventory"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "73-settings-and-pitfalls", "t": "Settings and pitfalls", "s": "User guide · Querying scans with DuckDB", "x": "Use UTC. Timestamps are stored in UTC, but DuckDB shows them, and groups them with year() or date_trunc() , in the session's time zone. A file modified at 2009-02-01 12:00 UTC shows as 04:00 in Los Angeles. Near midnight on New Year's Day, that can move a file into a different year. Set UTC at the start of a session: SET TimeZone = 'UTC'; Cast sums before format_bytes . sum() of a BIGINT column returns a 128-bit HUGEINT in DuckDB, which format_bytes() doesn't accept: Binder Error: No function matches the given name and argument types 'format_bytes(HUGEINT)' Cast the sum: format_bytes(sum(size)::BIGINT) . A single column such as size works without a cast. Sort on numbers, not formatted text. In SELECT format_bytes(size) AS size ... ORDER BY size , the ORDER BY uses the formatted text, so 8.0 KiB sorts above 8.0 GiB . Give the table an alias and sort on its column, as the recipes below do:", "c": ["fs_entries", "fs_inventory"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "74-whats-in-a-row", "t": "What's in a row", "s": "User guide · Querying scans with DuckDB", "x": "Every column is described in the schema reference . The ones most queries use: Column Use path , parent_path , name , extension Finding things by location or name kind file , dir , symlink , fifo , socket , char , block , or unknown size , alloc_bytes Logical size, and space actually allocated on disk uid , gid , mode Owner, group, and permissions ( mode & 4095 is the permission bits) mtime , ctime , atime , btime Timestamps (see Timestamps ) depth , dir_id , parent_id Tree structure dev , ino , nlink Identity; equal (dev, ino) means hard links to one file dir_entries , dir_files , dir_bytes , ... A directory's immediate contents error Why a directory wasn't fully read", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "75-recipes", "t": "Recipes", "s": "User guide · Querying scans with DuckDB", "x": "Every recipe assumes the setup from 7.3 . The outputs are from the demo tree used throughout this guide. Totals by kind SELECT kind, count(*) AS entries, format_bytes(sum(size)::BIGINT) AS size, format_bytes(sum(alloc_bytes)::BIGINT) AS on_disk FROM fs_entries(getvariable('scan')) GROUP BY kind ORDER BY entries DESC; ┌─────────┬─────────┬──────────┬──────────┐ │ kind │ entries │ size │ on_disk │ │ varchar │ int64 │ varchar │ varchar │ ├─────────┼─────────┼──────────┼──────────┤ │ file │ 248 │ 44.0 GiB │ 17.7 MiB │ │ dir │ 36 │ 33.9 KiB │ 32.0 KiB │ │ symlink │ 1 │ 12 bytes │ 0 bytes │ └─────────┴─────────┴──────────┴──────────┘ size is what programs see, and on_disk is the space allocated. Here they differ hugely because the demo tree's large files are sparse. On real data, compression, deduplication, and tiering to tape or object storage also make on_disk smaller than size . Largest fil", "c": ["--resolve-ids", "fs_diff", "fs_diff_summary", "fs_entries", "fs_entries_csv", "fs_entries_named", "fs_errors", "fs_errors_csv", "fs_inventory", "fs_rollup", "fs_rollup_ages", "fs_users"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "8-performance-tuning", "t": "Performance tuning", "s": "User guide", "x": "", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "81-threads", "t": "Threads", "s": "User guide · Performance tuning", "x": "The crawl is limited by how fast the file system answers metadata requests, not by CPU. On a local disk with metadata cached, a few threads are enough. On network file systems each request waits on the network, so throughput grows with the number of requests in flight. With 0.5 ms per request, 64 threads can sustain about 128,000 entries per second until the server is the bottleneck. Use probe ( 5.5 ) to measure, and keep in mind that the file server is shared: past the suggested count, more threads add load for little gain. A single huge directory doesn't hold the crawl back. After the first 10,000 entries, the thread reading it hands batches of names to idle threads, which read their metadata in parallel.", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "82-fields-and-caching", "t": "Fields and caching", "s": "User guide · Performance tuning", "x": "--fields reads less metadata per file. On Lustre, --fields -size,-blocks avoids a round trip per file to the storage servers. --statx-dont-sync lets a network file system client answer from cached attributes. It's faster, but may be slightly stale.", "c": ["--fields", "--statx-dont-sync"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "83-memory", "t": "Memory", "s": "User guide · Performance tuning", "x": "Memory is bounded by default: Directories waiting to be read: up to 1 GiB in memory. Beyond that, they wait in a spill file on disk, read back once memory drains. With checkpoints (the default), it's checkpoint/spill in the scan directory, removed when the scan finishes; otherwise it's created next to the output and deleted from the directory at once, so it never lingers. Either way, it uses disk space while the scan runs. The manifest's totals.spilled counts directories that waited there. Output buffers: 64 MiB shared by all crawl threads, plus up to 128 MiB per Parquet row group being built. Memory doesn't grow with the size of the tree: in scans of generated trees of up to a billion entries, it leveled off early and stayed flat to the end. It grows with threads, more slowly than they do. Without a budget, measured on Linux with two Parquet writers, a scan's peak was about 750 MB with ", "c": ["--age-depth", "--detect-cycles", "--max-memory", "--threads", "--writers"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "84-checkpoints", "t": "Checkpoints", "s": "User guide · Performance tuning", "x": "Each checkpoint pauses the crawl while every thread finishes the directory it's reading (helping with very large directories meanwhile), and every open part file is committed and synced to disk. That's typically under a second, and longer while a very large directory is finished. At the default interval of 10 minutes, the cost is well under 1%; much shorter intervals slow a scan noticeably. Each checkpoint writes the directories waiting in memory to checkpoint/state , compressed: a few megabytes on most trees, more on very broad ones.", "c": []}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 3, "id": "85-output", "t": "Output", "s": "User guide · Performance tuning", "x": "Parquet with zstd level 3 is the best balance of size and speed. Raise the level ( --compression zstd:9 ) for smaller files at the cost of more CPU. Writers are added automatically, up to --writers , only when output backs up. Write output to a different file system from the one being scanned.", "c": ["--compression", "--writers"]}, {"p": "guide.html", "l": 2, "id": "9-troubleshooting", "t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "User guide", "x": "output directory ... is not empty : each scan needs a new directory. Include the date in the name. No files found that match the pattern \".../errors/*.parquet\" from fs_errors : the scan had no errors, so there is no errors table. Check totals.errors in the manifest first. format_bytes(HUGEINT) : cast the sum: format_bytes(sum(size)::BIGINT) ( 7.3 ). Dates are off by some hours, or files land in the wrong year : set SET TimeZone = 'UTC'; in DuckDB. size is null : the scan was run with --fields leaving size out. The manifest's options.fields lists what was read. verify says schema version is 1 : the scan was written by a build before schema version 2 (which made integer columns signed). Scan again with this release. Schema version 2 scans (fs-inventory 0.5.0 and earlier) still work with verify , rollup , and convert ; they lack only the symlink target columns. scan status is running : the ", "c": ["--checkpoint-interval", "--fields", "--resolve-ids", "--stall-timeout", "fs-inventory process", "fs-inventory resume", "fs_errors"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "entries", "t": "entries", "s": "Schema", "x": "One row per file, directory, or other entry. Column Type Null Description path VARCHAR Full path. Always valid UTF-8: bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 appear as \\xNN , and in CSV, control characters are escaped ( \\n , \\t , \\xNN ). parent_path VARCHAR Path of the containing directory; empty for / . name VARCHAR Final path component. For a scan root, the root's own name. extension VARCHAR yes Lowercased text after the last . of a name that isn't a directory. None for names starting or ending with . or extensions longer than 32 bytes. path_raw (text: path_b64 ) BLOB yes Exact path bytes, when path differs from them (every row with --raw-paths always ). Base64 in CSV and JSON Lines, as path_b64 . Exact bytes for any row: coalesce(path_raw, encode(path)) . name_flags INTEGER Bitmask of name properties that cause trouble; see Name flags . kind VARCHAR Entry type; see Kinds . depth INTEGER Levels ", "c": ["--detect-cycles", "--fields", "--raw-paths", "--stall-timeout", "--symlink-targets"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "errors", "t": "errors", "s": "Schema", "x": "One row per failed operation. Present only if there were any. Column Type Null Description path VARCHAR Path of the entry the operation was on, rendered like entries.path . path_raw (text: path_b64 ) BLOB yes Exact path bytes, when path differs from them. Base64 in CSV and JSON Lines, as path_b64 . op VARCHAR The failed operation: open , readdir , stat , verify (the directory was replaced during the scan), stall (no progress for --stall-timeout , so the scan gave up on the directory), readlink (reading a symlink's target, with --symlink-targets ), or internal . errno INTEGER yes The OS error number, when there is one. errno_name VARCHAR yes Symbolic name of errno , such as EACCES . message VARCHAR Human-readable description. time TIMESTAMPTZ When the error happened.", "c": ["--stall-timeout", "--symlink-targets"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "users", "t": "users", "s": "Schema", "x": "With --resolve-ids : one row per user ID in entries.uid , with its name. Written when the scan finishes. Column Type Null Description uid BIGINT A user ID that owns at least one entry. name VARCHAR yes The user's name, as the scanning host looked it up when the scan finished; null when no account has that ID (such as a deleted user).", "c": ["--resolve-ids"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "groups", "t": "groups", "s": "Schema", "x": "With --resolve-ids : one row per group ID in entries.gid , with its name. Written when the scan finishes. Column Type Null Description gid BIGINT A group ID that owns at least one entry. name VARCHAR yes The group's name, as the scanning host looked it up when the scan finished; null when no group has that ID.", "c": ["--resolve-ids"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "rollup", "t": "rollup", "s": "Schema", "x": "Written by fs-inventory rollup : one row per directory with totals over its whole subtree. Parquet only. Column Type Null Description dir_id BIGINT The directory's dir_id in entries . parent_id BIGINT dir_id of the containing directory; 0 for a root. depth INTEGER Levels below the scan root; 0 for the root. path VARCHAR Full path, rendered like entries.path . path_raw (text: path_b64 ) BLOB yes Exact path bytes, when path differs from them. entries BIGINT Entries anywhere below this directory, not counting the directory itself. files BIGINT Entries below this directory that aren't directories. dirs BIGINT Directories below this directory. bytes BIGINT Sum of size over entries below this directory that aren't directories. alloc_bytes BIGINT Sum of alloc_bytes over entries below this directory that aren't directories. oldest_mtime TIMESTAMPTZ yes Oldest mtime below this directory, over ent", "c": ["--max-depth", "fs-inventory rollup"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "rollup_ages", "t": "rollup_ages", "s": "Schema", "x": "Written by fs-inventory rollup : file counts and bytes by age for each directory down to --age-depth , measured from the scan's start time. Parquet only. Column Type Null Description dir_id BIGINT The directory's dir_id ; join with rollup or entries for its path. time VARCHAR Which timestamp the age is measured from: mtime , ctime , or atime . bucket INTEGER Age bucket, in order: 0 is future (newer than the scan), then increasing age. label VARCHAR The bucket's label, such as <30d , 1y-2y , or >=10y , from --age-buckets . files BIGINT Entries below the directory in this bucket that aren't directories. Only buckets with files have rows. bytes BIGINT Sum of size over those entries.", "c": ["--age-buckets", "--age-depth", "fs-inventory rollup"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "name-flags", "t": "Name flags", "s": "Schema", "x": "Bits of name_flags . Decode them in DuckDB with fs_name_flags(name_flags) from sql/fs_inventory.sql . Bit Name Meaning 1 invalid_utf8 Not valid UTF-8: a legacy encoding (Latin-1, Shift-JIS, ...) or corruption 2 c0_control Contains an ASCII control character, such as a newline or tab 4 c1_control Contains a C1 control character (U+0080 to U+009F) 8 del Contains DEL (U+007F) 16 bidi_control Contains a bidirectional formatting character that can disguise how a name displays 32 not_nfc Valid UTF-8 but not in Unicode Normalization Form C (common from macOS) 64 leading_trailing_space Starts or ends with a space 128 leading_dash Starts with - , which tools can mistake for an option 256 trailing_dot Ends with . , which Windows strips 512 windows_reserved Contains a character Windows forbids ( <>:\"|?*\\ ) or is a device name such as CON 1024 long_name Longer than 255 bytes 2048 long_path The full ", "c": ["fs_inventory", "fs_name_flags"]}, {"p": "schema.html", "l": 2, "id": "kinds", "t": "Kinds", "s": "Schema", "x": "Values of kind . Kind Meaning file Regular file dir Directory symlink Symbolic link (never followed) fifo Named pipe socket Unix domain socket char Character device block Block device unknown Any other type", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "1-overview", "t": "Overview", "s": "Architecture", "x": "fs-inventory is a Cargo workspace with two crates: Crate Contents fs-inventory-core (library) The parallel walker, the platform backends, file-name analysis, and the record model. It knows nothing about output formats. fs-inventory (binary) The command-line interface, the output pipeline (CSV, JSON Lines, Parquet), the manifest, and the rollup , verify , schema , and probe commands. The boundary between them is the Sink trait in crates/fs-inventory-core/src/walker.rs . The walker calls entry , error , end_dir , and finish on one sink per crawl thread. Everything after that, including buffering, encoding, compression, and files, belongs to the binary. A scan flows like this: flowchart TB R[Roots] --> I[(Injector)] subgraph Crawl[\"Crawl threads (--threads)\"] W1[\"Worker 1: LIFO deque\"] W2[\"Worker 2: LIFO deque\"] end I --> W1 I --> W2 W2 -. steal .-> W1 W1 -- \"names past SHARE_AFTER\" --> C[(", "c": ["--threads", "--writers", "fs-inventory is"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "2-the-crawl", "t": "The crawl", "s": "Architecture", "x": "The walker is in crates/fs-inventory-core/src/walker.rs .", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "21-directory-tasks-and-ids", "t": "Directory tasks and IDs", "s": "Architecture · The crawl", "x": "A directory waiting to be read is a DirTask : its full path as bytes, dir_id , parent_id , depth, the stat its parent already took, and whether it is a mount point. The calling thread stats each root and pushes root directories onto a global Injector . A root that is a file gets a row immediately. A root that can't be stat'ed, such as a missing one, gets a row in the errors table. dir_id comes from one shared atomic counter, starting at 1. A directory gets its ID when its parent lists it, before it is queued. Every directory has an ID, including ones the crawl doesn't enter (mount points, directories at --max-depth , revisits). Roots have parent_id 0. IDs are dense, which rollup relies on to index directories in a flat array.", "c": ["--max-depth"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "22-work-stealing", "t": "Work stealing", "s": "Architecture · The crawl", "x": "Each crawl thread owns a LIFO deque from crossbeam-deque . It pushes the subdirectories it finds onto its own deque and pops the newest first, so each thread walks depth-first and its queue stays small. Each worker's loop, in order: Pop from its own deque. Help stat a batch from a huge directory (section 2.4). Steal: first a batch from the global injector, then from peers one at a time, starting with the next worker so stealing spreads evenly. Thieves take from the old end of a peer's deque, where the directories nearest the root are: the biggest remaining pieces of work. The loop stops at the first successful steal, so a stolen task is never dropped. Read back a directory from the spill file (section 6.3). If every directory is done, exit. Otherwise back off: spin, yield, then sleep 200 µs. Counters::pending counts directories queued or being read. The walk is finished when pending drop", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "23-reading-one-directory", "t": "Reading one directory", "s": "Architecture · The crawl", "x": "WorkerState::process opens the directory and checks that the open directory's device and inode match the stat taken when it was listed. If they don't, the directory was replaced during the scan, and it is recorded as a verify error instead of being read. A directory that vanished between listing and opening counts as vanished, not as an error. read_entries reads names in batches from the backend. For each name, stat_names takes a stat relative to the open directory, and child decides what to do: Snapshot directories ( .snapshot , ~snapshot , .snapshots , .zfs ; SNAPSHOT_DIRS ) and the scan's own output directory (matched by device and inode) are skipped entirely, with no row, unless --include-snapshots is set. Excluded paths ( --exclude , --exclude-from ) are skipped entirely, with no row, and excluded directories are not entered. A glob without / matches a name at any depth; it is compi", "c": ["--cross-mounts", "--detect-cycles", "--exclude", "--exclude-from", "--include-snapshots", "--max-depth"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "24-huge-directories", "t": "Huge directories", "s": "Architecture · The crawl", "x": "A single directory can hold millions of entries. Reading names ( getdents64 ) is one sequential stream, but the stat calls don't have to be. After a worker has read SHARE_AFTER (10,000) names from one directory, it stops statting the rest itself. Each further batch of names goes into a global StatChunk injector, together with an Arc<SharedDir> . The SharedDir holds the open directory, a mutex for totals, and a count of outstanding batches. Idle workers check this injector before they try to steal directories (step 2 above). They stat a batch relative to the shared open directory, emit its rows into their own sinks, and merge their totals. The reading worker keeps reading names. When it reaches the end, it helps with outstanding batches, its own or any other directory's, until all of its directory's batches are done. Then it merges the shared totals and writes the directory row. A drop gu", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "25-containing-failures", "t": "Containing failures", "s": "Architecture · The crawl", "x": "Every entry is processed inside catch_unwind . A bug triggered by one strange entry costs that entry: it is recorded as an internal error, and the rest of the directory is still read. The same applies to each directory as a whole. Stat failures are recorded in the errors table, and the crawl continues. Entries that disappear between being listed and being stat'ed are counted as vanished, not as errors, because live file systems churn.", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "26-stopping", "t": "Stopping", "s": "Architecture · The crawl", "x": "The walk stops early when its stop flag is set. That happens on the first SIGINT or SIGTERM, or when output fails (section 5.6). Workers check the flag between directories, so each finishes the directory it is reading. The directories still queued are left unread, and the walk reports itself incomplete. As they exit, workers move their queued directories to the global injector, so the walk can return them as the state a resume continues from (section 6.5).", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "3-platform-backends", "t": "Platform backends", "s": "Architecture", "x": "The walker only uses the Backend trait ( crates/fs-inventory-core/src/backend/mod.rs ): Method Purpose stat_root Stat a root, following a symlink at the root itself open_dir Open a directory by full path, following a final symlink only for roots dir_stat Stat an open directory (for the replaced-directory check) read_names Append the next batch of names, never . or .. stat_at Stat a name relative to an open directory, without following symlinks or triggering automounts read_link_at Read a symlink's target relative to an open directory ( readlinkat ), for --symlink-targets An open directory ( Backend::Dir ) must be shareable across threads, because huge directories are stat'ed by several workers at once. Tests wrap the native backend to inject failures, trace calls, or hang (section 9), and scale tests replace it with a generated tree (section 3.4).", "c": ["--symlink-targets"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "31-linux", "t": "Linux", "s": "Architecture · Platform backends", "x": "crates/fs-inventory-core/src/backend/linux.rs : Listing: getdents64 through rustix's RawDir , into a 256 KiB buffer per worker. Each read_names call issues exactly one getdents64 . Metadata: statx relative to the open directory, with AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW and AT_NO_AUTOMOUNT . The field mask comes from --fields . Fields left out aren't requested at all, which on Lustre avoids a round trip to the storage servers for sizes. --statx-dont-sync adds AT_STATX_DONT_SYNC , which accepts cached attributes on network file systems. Kernels before 4.11 have no statx . The first ENOSYS is remembered in an atomic flag, and every later stat calls fstatat directly, with the same flags except AT_STATX_DONT_SYNC . btime is then null. Access times: directories are opened with O_NOATIME where permitted, so reading them doesn't update their access times. O_NOATIME needs ownership or CAP_FOWNER ; after the firs", "c": ["--fields", "--statx-dont-sync"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "32-macos-and-other-posix-systems", "t": "macOS and other POSIX systems", "s": "Architecture · Platform backends", "x": "crates/fs-inventory-core/src/backend/posix.rs uses readdir (rustix Dir ), 4,096 names per call, and fstatat relative to the open directory. Birth time comes from st_birthtime on Apple platforms and the BSDs. Every metadata field is read regardless of --fields ; the output still writes skipped fields as null.", "c": ["--fields"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "33-long-paths", "t": "Long paths", "s": "Architecture · Platform backends", "x": "Paths can exceed PATH_MAX (4,096 bytes on Linux, 1,024 on macOS), and open then fails with ENAMETOOLONG . DirOpener retries by splitting the path at / into pieces shorter than PATH_MAX and opening each with openat relative to the one before, applying the caller's flags only to the final piece. An 8,000-byte path takes two or three calls on Linux, rather than one per component, which made opening every directory of a 2,000-level tree cost millions of calls. Paths are stored in full regardless of length, and name_flags marks them with long_path .", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "34-generated-trees", "t": "Generated trees", "s": "Architecture · Platform backends", "x": "crates/fs-inventory-core/src/backend/synthetic.rs serves a tree that exists only as arithmetic, for scale tests and benchmarks. The hidden option scan --synthetic SPEC uses it in place of the native backend at each root, and the roots needn't exist. The spec is 100M , or entries=100M,files=32,fanout=8,huge=1M,seed=0 . The checkpoint stores it, so resume continues the same tree, and the manifest's options.synthetic marks the scan as generated. Exact size: each directory has a budget, the number of entries below it. A hash of the directory decides how many of them are its own files and how many subdirectories share the rest, unevenly. Files are about files per directory, now and then up to 32 times that, and huge one time in four when at least twice huge entries are below. Subdirectories are about fanout . The budgets add up, so each root's tree has exactly entries entries, the root includ", "c": ["--synthetic"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "4-file-names", "t": "File names", "s": "Architecture", "x": "On Unix, a file name is any sequence of bytes except / and NUL. Names from legacy systems are often Latin-1 or Shift-JIS rather than UTF-8, and some contain newlines or other control characters. fs-inventory keeps names as bytes from getdents64 to the output boundary, and converts them to text only when writing a row ( crates/fs-inventory-core/src/names.rs ). to_text returns valid UTF-8 unchanged. Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 become \\xNN escapes. For CSV, control characters are also escaped ( \\t , \\n , \\r , \\xNN ), so every record stays on one line; --csv-raw-controls turns that off. JSON Lines and Parquet keep control characters, because those formats escape or store them safely. Exact bytes: to_text returns a borrowed string exactly when the text equals the original bytes. When it doesn't, the writer also stores the exact bytes: path_raw (binary) in Parquet, path_b64 (base64) in CSV a", "c": ["--csv-raw-controls", "--raw-paths", "fs-inventory keeps"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "5-output-pipeline", "t": "Output pipeline", "s": "Architecture", "x": "The pipeline is in crates/fs-inventory/src/output/ .", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "51-sinks", "t": "Sinks", "s": "Architecture · Output pipeline", "x": "Each crawl thread gets a RowSink . A sink buffers rows as text (CSV or JSON Lines) or in Arrow column builders (Parquet). The expensive Parquet encoding and compression happen later, in the writers. The buffer lives in a SinkState behind an Arc<Mutex<_>> , which the output also keeps so it can salvage a stuck worker's rows (section 6.2). Each sink lock is uncontended in normal operation. Sinks send rows at directory boundaries. At each end_dir , the sink records a mark: the rows up to here belong to finished directories. It sends its buffer once the buffer passes its share of the sink budget: Setting Value Sink budget for all threads together SINK_BUDGET_BYTES , 64 MiB (usually less with --max-memory ) One thread's share budget ÷ crawl threads, clamped to 64 KiB through 1 MiB Mid-directory send when one directory's rows reach 4 × the share ( MAX_BUFFER_FACTOR ) Error rows sent at a direc", "c": ["--max-memory"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "52-writers", "t": "Writers", "s": "Architecture · Output pipeline", "x": "Sinks send chunks through a bounded crossbeam channel that holds BACKLOG_PER_WRITER (4) chunks per allowed writer. When the channel is full, sinks block, and the crawl slows to the speed of the disk instead of growing memory. Writers start on demand. The output starts with one writer. When a sink sends a chunk and finds at least 4 chunks queued per running writer, it starts another, up to --writers (default: the CPU count, at most 8). The last rows each sink sends when it finishes don't start writers: all the sinks finish at about the same moment, so their final chunks arrive together even when the writers are keeping up. A small scan therefore writes one part file per table, and a large one uses as many writers as compression needs. Each writer has its own part files for each table, named part-wNN-SSSSSS (writer, then sequence). Output files therefore scale with --writers , not --thread", "c": ["--threads", "--writers"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "53-part-files-and-the-commit-protocol", "t": "Part files and the commit protocol", "s": "Architecture · Output pipeline", "x": "A writer rolls to a new part at --part-max-bytes (default 1 GiB, measured after compression) or --part-max-rows . A chunk is never split, so its rows always land in one part. Each part is committed in three steps ( crates/fs-inventory/src/output/parts.rs and parquet.rs ): Write NAME.tmp ; for Parquet, the footer is written last. fsync the file. Rename it to NAME . A finished part is therefore always complete and valid, and a crash leaves only .tmp files behind. Zstd text parts include a content checksum. At the end of the scan, the manifest is written the same way ( manifest.json.tmp , fsync, rename), and the scan directory itself is fsynced so the renames are durable.", "c": ["--part-max-bytes", "--part-max-rows"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "54-parquet-settings", "t": "Parquet settings", "s": "Architecture · Output pipeline", "x": "crates/fs-inventory/src/output/parquet.rs configures the writer: Compression: zstd level 3 by default; --compression also accepts other zstd or gzip levels, Snappy, LZ4, or none. Row groups: up to --row-group-rows (default 1,048,576) rows. An in-progress row group is also capped in bytes: MAX_ROW_GROUP_BYTES (128 MiB) per writer by default, and less under --max-memory , but never under 8 MiB. Statistics: page-level, so DuckDB can skip data by mtime , size , or uid . Encodings, chosen by measurement on 1.24 million real entries: path uses DELTA_BYTE_ARRAY , storing each path as a suffix of the previous one, which cut it from 7.9 to 5.0 bytes per entry. ino uses DELTA_BINARY_PACKED . Dictionary encoding is off for high-cardinality columns ( path , name , path_raw , ino , dir_id , and the timestamps), where it made files larger and slower. Footer metadata: every file records fs_inventory.sc", "c": ["--columns", "--compression", "--max-memory", "--row-group-rows", "fs-inventory schema", "fs_inventory"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "55-text-formats-and-stdout", "t": "Text formats and stdout", "s": "Architecture · Output pipeline", "x": "CSV and JSON Lines rows are encoded in the sinks ( output/encode.rs ) and written by the same rolling part writers, optionally through zstd or gzip. Timestamps are ISO-8601 UTC text by default, or epoch seconds with --time-format epoch , in microseconds or nanoseconds ( --time-unit ). A timestamp a format can't represent is clamped, and time_flags records which ones were clamped. With -o - , there is one writer, entry rows stream to stdout as CSV or JSON Lines, and error rows go to stderr. No manifest is written. A closed pipe, such as | head , ends the scan cleanly with exit code 3.", "c": ["--time-format", "--time-unit"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "56-failures-and-the-manifest", "t": "Failures and the manifest", "s": "Architecture · Output pipeline", "x": "If a writer fails, for example because the disk is full, it records the error, sets the walk's stop flag, and drains the channel so no sink blocks forever. The scan ends with status failed and exit code 4. Parts committed before the failure are listed and valid. The manifest ( output/manifest.rs ) records: the scan ID, a time-ordered UUIDv7; the tool and schema versions, host, roots, format, compression, columns, and the options that shape the output; start and finish times; totals: entries by kind, directories read, bytes, errors, vanished, revisits, spilled; errors grouped by operation and errno; stalled directories; each resume, if any ( resumes ); every committed part, with its row count and size. Its status is one of: Status Meaning running Written at the start. Still present after a crash, when resume continues from the last checkpoint. complete Every directory was visited. Per-ent", "c": ["--stall-timeout"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "57-user-and-group-names", "t": "User and group names", "s": "Architecture · Output pipeline", "x": "With --resolve-ids , crates/fs-inventory/src/ids.rs adds two small tables, users and groups : Collecting: each sink keeps an IdCollector , a set of the user and group IDs of its rows. Entries in a directory usually share an owner, so the collector skips a pair equal to the last one with a single comparison. A checkpoint merges every sink's IDs, with those of earlier runs, into its header, so a resumed scan's tables cover the whole scan. Looking up: once the scan finishes (complete or incomplete, not interrupted), each unique ID is looked up once. getent passwd and getent group take up to 1,000 IDs per call and print the ones that exist; through the host's name service switch they reach LDAP, sssd, and NIS, which the static binary can't load itself. Where there's no getent (macOS), dscacheutil answers one ID per call through Directory Services. Each command gets 60 seconds; one that times", "c": ["--resolve-ids"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "6-robustness", "t": "Robustness", "s": "Architecture", "x": "", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "61-errors-and-vanished-entries", "t": "Errors and vanished entries", "s": "Architecture · Robustness", "x": "Per-entry failures go to the errors table, with the path, the failed operation ( open , readdir , stat , verify , stall , or internal ), the errno and its name, and a message. Directories that failed also carry the error in their own entries row. A revisit ( --detect-cycles ) isn't a failure: it appears only in the directory's own row, as revisit: ... in error , and in the manifest's revisits count. Workers keep their counts locally and add them to the shared counters once per directory. The manifest summarizes errors by operation and errno. Deletions aren't failures. An entry deleted between being listed and being stat'ed, or a directory deleted before it's opened, counts as vanished. So does a directory deleted while it's being read, which Linux reports as ENOENT from getdents64 : a directory can only be deleted once it's empty, so nothing that remained unread is missing, and its row i", "c": ["--detect-cycles"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "62-the-stall-watchdog", "t": "The stall watchdog", "s": "Architecture · Robustness", "x": "A thread stuck in a system call to a hung NFS server can't be interrupted. The walker can't free that thread, but it can stop waiting for it. The calling thread of walk is the watchdog. It wakes every WATCHDOG_TICK (250 ms), or as soon as a worker exits. Each worker has a WorkerStatus with two fields: a progress word: 0 while idle, otherwise the time of its last progress in milliseconds since the walk started; the directory or batch it is working on. Workers update the progress word after every stat and every batch of names, and before writing a directory's row. Each update is a compare-and-swap that fails if the watchdog has stored ABANDONED ( u64::MAX ). A worker that sees that knows to stop and write nothing more. Workers don't read the system clock for these updates. The watchdog refreshes a coarse clock on each tick, kept in its own cache line, and workers read it from memory. Readi", "c": ["--stall-timeout"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "63-bounded-memory", "t": "Bounded memory", "s": "Architecture · Robustness", "x": "Every queued directory holds memory: about the size of a DirTask plus its path. A broad tree can queue millions. The walker tracks the total in queued_bytes . When adding a directory would push it past the queue budget (1 GiB by default), the directory goes to a spill file instead ( crates/fs-inventory-core/src/spill.rs ): With checkpoints, the spill file is checkpoint/spill in the scan directory, so a checkpoint can refer to it (section 6.5); the scan removes it when it finishes. Without checkpoints, it's created next to the output (or in the temporary directory when writing to stdout) and unlinked immediately, so it never outlives the process, even after a crash. Records are length-prefixed and read first in, first out. Workers read spilled directories back only when they find no other work: nothing in their own deque, no huge-directory batches, and nothing to steal. If the spill file ", "c": ["--age-depth", "--detect-cycles", "--max-memory", "--writers"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "64-signals-and-file-descriptors", "t": "Signals and file descriptors", "s": "Architecture · Robustness", "x": "The first SIGINT or SIGTERM sets the stop flag. Workers finish their current directories, the writers drain and commit, a final checkpoint is written, and the manifest is written with status interrupted . A second signal exits immediately with code 130. At startup, scan raises the open-file limit, since every crawl thread holds a directory open.", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "65-checkpoints-and-resume", "t": "Checkpoints and resume", "s": "Architecture · Robustness", "x": "A billion-entry scan can run for a day, so it has to survive being killed. The design rests on one property: when every worker is between directories, every row delivered to a sink belongs to a finished directory, and every directory not yet finished is in a queue. A checkpoint waits for that moment, makes the rows durable, and records the queue. Taking a checkpoint. The walker's calling thread, which is also the watchdog, starts one every --checkpoint-interval (10 minutes by default; crates/fs-inventory-core/src/walker.rs ): It sets a pause flag. Each worker checks it before taking its next directory, moves its own deque to the global injector, adds its counts to the shared totals, and waits. Paused workers keep helping with huge-directory batches (section 2.4), since the worker reading a huge directory can't pause until its batches are done. While a paused worker runs a batch, it doesn", "c": ["--checkpoint-interval", "--detect-cycles", "--exclude-from", "--stall-timeout", "fs-inventory resume"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "7-post-processing-commands", "t": "Post-processing commands", "s": "Architecture", "x": "", "c": []}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "71-rollup", "t": "rollup", "s": "Architecture · Post-processing commands", "x": "Each directory row carries totals for its immediate children. rollup ( crates/fs-inventory/src/rollup.rs ) computes totals over whole subtrees, plus file-age histograms, from a finished Parquet scan. It reads only the columns it needs, in three passes: Directories: kind , dir_id , parent_id , depth , dir_entries , error . Directories go into an array indexed by dir_id . A directory counts as incomplete when it has an error (other than revisit ) or no dir_entries (not read). The directories are then sorted by depth, and each one down to --age-depth gets a histogram slot. Deeper directories share their ancestor's slot. Every row: parent_id , kind , size , alloc_bytes , and the three timestamps. Each row adds to its parent's totals and to the histogram of its parent's slot. The histogram is by age bucket, for mtime , ctime , and atime . Ages are measured from the scan's start time, and buck", "c": ["--age-depth", "--columns"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "72-verify", "t": "verify", "s": "Architecture · Post-processing commands", "x": "verify ( crates/fs-inventory/src/verify.rs ) checks a scan before anything ingests it: The manifest can be read and has a schema version this build reads: the current one or an older one it understands ( readable_schema in fs-inventory-core ). The status is complete . Every listed part exists and has the size the manifest records. Every part has the row count the manifest records. For Parquet that comes from the footer. For text it comes from counting lines, after decompressing if needed, except CSV written with --csv-raw-controls , whose records can span lines. Every Parquet part's footer has this scan's ID, so parts from different scans can't be mixed. No unlisted files sit in a table directory, such as leftover .tmp files. The entry and error row counts across parts match the manifest totals. The rollup tables are checked the same way when present. verify exits 0 when everything check", "c": ["--csv-raw-controls"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "73-schema-and-the-duckdb-macros", "t": "schema and the DuckDB macros", "s": "Architecture · Post-processing commands", "x": "fs-inventory schema prints the schema as DuckDB SQL, JSON, or Markdown from the same column definitions the writers use. The schema version changes when columns are added or change. Version 3 added symlink_target and symlink_target_raw at the end of the entries table, so the CSV macro reads version 2 scans too, filling the missing trailing columns with null ( null_padding ). verify , rollup , and convert read versions 2 and 3; resume needs the version it writes, so a resumed scan's parts all match. docs/schema.md is that Markdown. Unit tests in output/schema.rs fail if docs/schema.md is out of date, or if the column lists in the CSV and JSON Lines macros in sql/fs_inventory.sql differ from the schema. Another test checks that only dev , ino , and rdev are unsigned. Comparing two scans is a macro too, not a subcommand. fs_diff joins the entries of two Parquet scans on their exact path byt", "c": ["fs-inventory schema", "fs_diff", "fs_inventory"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "74-probe", "t": "probe", "s": "Architecture · Post-processing commands", "x": "probe ( crates/fs-inventory/src/probe.rs ) runs the real walker with a sink that discards rows, once per thread count. A timer thread sets the stop flag after --duration . It reports entries per second for each count and suggests the smallest thread count within 90% of the best rate. On Linux, --drop-caches (root only) syncs and drops the page, dentry, and inode caches before each run, so later runs don't read metadata cached by earlier ones.", "c": ["--drop-caches", "--duration"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 3, "id": "75-convert", "t": "convert", "s": "Architecture · Post-processing commands", "x": "convert ( crates/fs-inventory/src/convert.rs ) re-exports a Parquet scan as CSV or JSON Lines. Rather than formatting Arrow values itself, it turns each Parquet row back into the walker's Entry and hands it to the same Encoder a text scan uses, so the output equals what that scan would have written. The integration tests check this byte for byte, for every encoding option. Exact bytes: a row's path bytes are path_raw when set, else the path text (which equals the bytes then). The parent path and name are split from those bytes with the walker's own functions, and symlink targets come from symlink_target_raw the same way. Settings: fields, column selection, raw-path and control-character rules, time unit, and time format come from the manifest's options . A time_flags bit set in the Parquet scan (a time clamped to the nanosecond range) is kept. Missing columns: scans made with --columns l", "c": ["--columns"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "8-performance-notes", "t": "Performance notes", "s": "Architecture", "x": "Thread count: on network file systems, each stat waits on a round trip, so throughput is roughly concurrency divided by latency (Little's law). With 0.5 ms metadata calls, 64 stats in flight can sustain about 128,000 per second until the server saturates. Network file systems want many more threads (64–256) than local disks. probe measures this on the actual storage. Allocator: the binary uses mimalloc ( crates/fs-inventory/src/alloc.rs ). Crawl threads allocate row buffers constantly, and musl's allocator serialized them on a lock, so the static Linux build got slower as threads were added. mimalloc's default of returning freed memory a second late is kept for speed, except under --max-memory (section 6.3). System calls: on Linux, rustix makes system calls directly, so the musl and glibc builds stat files the same way. The remaining per-entry libc dependency, reading the clock, was remo", "c": ["--max-memory", "--writers"]}, {"p": "architecture.html", "l": 2, "id": "9-testing", "t": "Testing", "s": "Architecture", "x": "Tests are in each crate's tests/ directory and in unit tests next to the code. Oracle comparison: walker tests and scan tests build trees and compare every path and its metadata against a walk made with Rust's standard library. They run at several thread counts, for JSON Lines and Parquet output, and also check that every row's parent_id links to its parent's dir_id and that directory totals are correct. Fault injection: test backends wrap the native one. FaultyBackend injects EACCES , EIO , ESTALE , vanishing entries, and a panic on chosen names. The tests assert that the crawl never stops and the errors table is right. TracingBackend records which thread stats each name, to show huge directories are shared. HangingBackend blocks on a chosen directory, to show the watchdog abandons it and the scan finishes. Other tests cover spilling with a tiny queue budget and --detect-cycles . Output", "c": ["--columns", "--detect-cycles", "--fields"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 2, "id": "unreleased", "t": "Unreleased", "s": "Changelog", "x": "", "c": []}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 2, "id": "100---2026-10-26", "t": "1.0.0 - 2026-10-26", "s": "Changelog", "x": "Compare two scans with the fs_diff DuckDB macros, much faster scans of very deep trees, and directories deleted while they're read counted as vanished instead of as errors. New tests check the output against GNU find , scans of a tree that changes throughout, random names through every text encoder, and trees 2,000 levels deep or with a million entries in one directory. From 1.0 on, the command line, the output tables and their columns, the manifest, and the DuckDB macros follow semantic versioning: 1.x releases can add options, columns (at the end of a table, with a new schema_version ), and macros, but won't remove or rename them.", "c": ["fs_diff"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "added", "t": "Added", "s": "Changelog · 1.0.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "fs_diff and fs_diff_summary DuckDB macros compare two Parquet scans of the same roots: one row per added, removed, or changed entry, with the columns that changed and the rows before and after, or the counts and net size change by kind. Entries are matched by their exact path bytes; atime and dev are ignored unless ignore := [] is given. They work on version 2 scans too.", "c": ["fs_diff", "fs_diff_summary"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "changed", "t": "Changed", "s": "Changelog · 1.0.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "convert takes its output format with -f / --format , like scan . --to , its name in 0.6.0, still works.", "c": ["--format", "--to"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "fixed", "t": "Fixed", "s": "Changelog · 1.0.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "Directories whose paths are longer than PATH_MAX (4,096 bytes on Linux, 1,024 on macOS) are opened in a few calls instead of one call per path component. Opening every directory of a 2,000-level tree took millions of calls: scanning one on macOS went from 7.5 minutes to 6 seconds. A directory deleted while it was being read counts as vanished, like other deletions during a scan, instead of as a readdir error (Linux reports it as ENOENT ). Busy file systems no longer end a scan with exit code 1 for routine churn.", "c": []}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 2, "id": "060---2026-10-26", "t": "0.6.0 - 2026-10-26", "s": "Changelog", "x": "Re-export Parquet scans as CSV or JSON Lines with convert , record symlink targets, and look up user and group names.", "c": []}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "added-1", "t": "Added", "s": "Changelog · 0.6.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "fs-inventory convert re-exports a Parquet scan as CSV or JSON Lines, into a new scan directory or to stdout, with optional zstd or gzip compression. The output equals what the scan would have written in that format, using the scan's own column, encoding, and time settings. Older (schema version 2) scans convert too. --resolve-ids writes users and groups tables with the name of every user and group ID in a scan (null for IDs with no account, such as deleted users). Crawl threads collect the IDs as they go, and each is looked up once when the scan finishes: with getent on Linux, which reaches LDAP, sssd, and NIS even from the static binary, and dscacheutil on macOS. Checkpoints keep the IDs, so resumed scans get complete tables. New DuckDB macros: fs_users , fs_groups , and fs_entries_named , which adds user_name and group_name to every entry. --symlink-targets records where each symlink p", "c": ["--resolve-ids", "--symlink-targets", "fs-inventory convert", "fs_entries_named", "fs_groups", "fs_users"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "changed-1", "t": "Changed", "s": "Changelog · 0.6.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "Schema version 3: the entries table gains symlink_target and symlink_target_raw , at the end, so the CSV and JSON Lines macros read version 2 scans too. verify , rollup , and convert accept version 2 scans; resume continues only scans of the current version.", "c": []}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 2, "id": "050---2026-10-26", "t": "0.5.0 - 2026-10-26", "s": "Changelog", "x": "Release binaries and an installer, a documentation website, scale tests up to a billion entries, and a --max-memory that budgets the whole scan.", "c": ["--max-memory"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "added-2", "t": "Added", "s": "Changelog · 0.5.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "Release binaries: each release has static Linux binaries (x86_64 and ARM) and macOS binaries (Apple silicon and Intel), with a SHA256SUMS file. Pushing a version tag builds them and drafts the release. A documentation website, https://robert-mcdermott.github.io/fs-inventory/ , generated from the repository's Markdown: the user guide, schema, architecture, and changelog, with navigation, search across every page, copy buttons, and dark mode. Publishing a release rebuilds it from that release's docs, and CI fails a pull request that breaks a link between pages or sections. install.sh installs a release with curl ... | sh : it verifies the SHA-256 checksum and the binary's version before installing to ~/.local/bin (or --install-dir ), and never uses sudo or changes PATH . Generated trees for scale tests: the hidden option scan --synthetic 100M scans a deterministic tree computed in memory i", "c": ["--install-dir", "--synthetic"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "changed-2", "t": "Changed", "s": "Changelog · 0.5.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "--max-memory budgets the whole scan. It first sets aside what the crawl threads and Parquet writers need (memory grows with threads, measured at about 1.7 GB with 64 threads and 3 GB with 256 without a budget), uses only as many writers as fit unless --writers is given, and shrinks row buffers as threads are added. It also has the allocator return freed memory at once, which cut peak memory by 40 to 50% on Linux for 5 to 8% more CPU time. A budget too small for the threads gets a warning with the budget they need. Scans without --max-memory run exactly as before.", "c": ["--max-memory", "--writers"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "removed", "t": "Removed", "s": "Changelog · 0.5.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "--guess-encoding , which was never implemented. Guesses of a name's legacy character set are unreliable on short names, and the exact bytes of every name are kept, so they can be decoded later with the right character set.", "c": ["--guess-encoding"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "fixed-1", "t": "Fixed", "s": "Changelog · 0.5.0 - 2026-10-26", "x": "The benchmark scripts run on Python 3.6 and later, as RHEL 8's system Python is; run.py needed 3.9.", "c": []}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 2, "id": "040---2026-10-25", "t": "0.4.0 - 2026-10-25", "s": "Changelog", "x": "Milestone M4: resumable scans.", "c": []}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "added-3", "t": "Added", "s": "Changelog · 0.4.0 - 2026-10-25", "x": "Checkpoints and fs-inventory resume . Every --checkpoint-interval (10 minutes by default; 0 disables them), a scan pauses as each crawl thread finishes its current directory, commits every open part file, and records the directories still to read in checkpoint/ inside the scan directory. fs-inventory resume SCAN_DIR continues a scan that was interrupted, killed, or on a machine that went down. It deletes the parts written after the last checkpoint and reads their directories again, so the finished scan matches an uninterrupted one, with every row once. After Ctrl-C, a final checkpoint makes resuming exact. Resume can change threads, writers, memory, the stall timeout, the checkpoint interval, and progress; the manifest records each resume under resumes . A lock keeps two processes from writing one scan.", "c": ["--checkpoint-interval", "fs-inventory resume"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "changed-3", "t": "Changed", "s": "Changelog · 0.4.0 - 2026-10-25", "x": "With checkpoints, the spill file and the --detect-cycles record of visited directories live in checkpoint/ until the scan finishes, instead of being unlinked at once. Each checkpoint commits the open part files, so long scans have more, smaller parts. Parts written after a resume are named part-rN-wNN-NNNNNN .", "c": ["--detect-cycles"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 2, "id": "030---2026-10-25", "t": "0.3.0 - 2026-10-25", "s": "Changelog", "x": "The first tagged release. Version numbers follow the milestones, so 0.3.0 completes milestone M3 and there is no 0.1.0 or 0.2.0 release.", "c": []}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "added-4", "t": "Added", "s": "Changelog · 0.3.0 - 2026-10-25", "x": "fs-inventory rollup SCAN_DIR writes two Parquet tables into the scan: rollup , with totals over every directory's whole subtree (entries, files, directories, bytes, allocated bytes, oldest and newest times, and how many directories weren't fully read), and rollup_ages , with file counts and bytes by age for directories down to --age-depth (default 4), in buckets set by --age-buckets (default 30d,90d,1y,2y,3y,5y,10y ) and measured from the scan's start time. The manifest lists both, verify checks them, and running it again replaces them. DuckDB macros fs_rollup and fs_rollup_ages , and heat-map examples in sql/examples.sql . --fields chooses which optional metadata to read (size, blocks, atime, mtime, ctime, btime). Skipped fields are written as null, and on Linux they aren't requested from the kernel, so --fields -size,-blocks avoids a round trip per file to Lustre's storage servers. siz", "c": ["--age-buckets", "--age-depth", "--columns", "--cross-mounts", "--detect-cycles", "--drop-caches", "--duration", "--fields", "--max-depth", "--max-memory", "--stall-timeout", "--threads", "fs-inventory command", "fs-inventory probe", "fs-inventory rollup", "fs-inventory schema"]}, {"p": "changelog.html", "l": 3, "id": "changed-4", "t": "Changed", "s": "Changelog · 0.3.0 - 2026-10-25", "x": "Writer threads start one at a time and are added, up to --writers , only when output backs up. Small scans now write one part file per table instead of one per CPU core. Schema version 2: integer columns are now signed ( INTEGER or BIGINT ), so scans load into databases without unsigned types and DuckDB functions such as format_bytes() work without casts. Only dev , ino , and rdev stay unsigned ( UBIGINT ). Values above the signed maximum are capped, not wrapped. verify reports scans written with schema version 1.", "c": ["--writers"]}];
